    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/sir-harry-smith-1st-baronet"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sir-harry-smith-1st-baronet.jpg" alt="Sir Harry Smith, 1st Baronet" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/sir-harry-smith-1st-baronet">Sir Harry Smith, 1st Baronet</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sir_Harry_Smith,_Bt.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown authorUnknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Orange Free State took shape on the high grasslands between the Orange and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaal-river">Vaal</a> rivers, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/voortrekkers">Voortrekkers</a> and other Dutch-speaking farmers from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-colony">Cape Colony</a> settled from the 1830s among <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/griqua">Griqua</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barolong">Barolong</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sotho-people">Sotho</a> and other communities. In February 1848 the Cape governor Sir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sir-harry-smith-1st-baronet">Harry Smith</a> proclaimed British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> over the region as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orange-river">Orange River</a> Sovereignty, and he defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boer</a> resistance under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andries-pretorius">Andries Pretorius</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-boomplaats">Battle of Boomplaats</a> on 29 August 1848. The Sovereignty proved costly and unruly, embroiled in land disputes between settlers and the Basotho of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moshoeshoe-i">Moshoeshoe</a>, and after damaging clashes with the Basotho at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-viervoet">Viervoet</a> in 1851 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-berea">Berea</a> in 1852 the government in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> decided to withdraw. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bloemfontein-convention">Bloemfontein Convention</a> of 23 February 1854, the special commissioner <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-russell-clerk">Sir George Clerk</a> handed the territory to representatives of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burghers">burghers</a>, some of whom had been reluctant to accept independence. The new state adopted a written constitution in April 1854 and kept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bloemfontein">Bloemfontein</a>, founded as a British post in 1846, as its capital. Its first president, Josias Hoffman, resigned in 1855 after a gift of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunpowder">gunpowder</a> to Moshoeshoe angered the burghers, and his successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jacobus-boshof">Jacobus Boshof</a> gave the republic firmer direction. In 1861 the state acquired the lands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adam-kok-iii">Adam Kok III</a>’s Griqua at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippolis">Philippolis</a>, whose people then trekked east to found Griqualand East.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/koffiefontein"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/koffiefontein.jpg" alt="Koffiefontein" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/koffiefontein">Koffiefontein</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Koffiefontein,_South_Africa_-_panoramio_(12).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Graham Maclachlan, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Free State was an overwhelmingly rural society of white farming families, African labour tenants and servants, and surviving African communities such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barolong">Barolong</a> chiefdom of Thaba Nchu, which kept its autonomy until 1884. Farms were large and the white population small, scattered around a handful of villages that served as district seats and church centres. Sheep farming for wool became the chief source of cash income from the 1850s, alongside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>, horses and, on the fertile lands of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caledon-river">Caledon</a> valley, wheat and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a>. Wool, hides and grain were carted to the ports of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-colony">Cape Colony</a> and Natal, so the republic depended on its neighbours for imports and in 1889 joined a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/customs-union">customs union</a> with the Cape. The discovery of diamonds in the region from 1867 brought markets and money, and the mines at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jagersfontein">Jagersfontein</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koffiefontein">Koffiefontein</a> lay within Free State territory. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> was the official language and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-reformed-church">Dutch Reformed Church</a> the centre of community life, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grey-college-bloemfontein">Grey College</a>, founded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bloemfontein">Bloemfontein</a> in 1855, and a growing number of state schools built a modest educational system. Black Africans had no political rights and were subject to pass regulations, and from the early 1890s legislation barred Asians from settling in the republic.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-magersfontein"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-magersfontein.jpg" alt="Battle of Magersfontein" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-magersfontein">Battle of Magersfontein</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:British_in_Barbed_Wire_Magersfontein.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown authorUnknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Like its northern neighbour, the Free State relied on the commando system: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burghers">burghers</a> of military age served without pay when called out by their field cornets and commandants, bringing their own horses, rifles and food. A small professional State Artillery was added later. The republic’s main wars were fought against the Basotho Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moshoeshoe-i">Moshoeshoe</a> over the fertile land west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caledon-river">Caledon River</a>. A first war in 1858 ended in a stalemate after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sotho-people">Basotho</a> raided deep into Free State districts. In the war of 1865–1866 the commandos failed to storm the mountain fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thaba-bosiu">Thaba Bosiu</a>, where the commandant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louw-wepener">Louw Wepener</a> was killed in 1865, but they devastated Basotho fields and herds and forced Moshoeshoe to cede land in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-thaba-bosiu">Treaty of Thaba Bosiu</a> in 1866. When fighting resumed in 1867, the Basotho faced collapse until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> annexed Basutoland in March 1868. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-boer-war">Second Boer War</a> the Free State mobilised its commandos alongside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transvaal">Transvaal</a> from October 1899, besieging <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kimberley">Kimberley</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ladysmith-kwazulu-natal">Ladysmith</a> and fighting at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-magersfontein">Magersfontein</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-paardeberg">Paardeberg</a>. After the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bloemfontein">Bloemfontein</a>, Christiaan de Wet became the most famous <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> leader of the war, winning at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sannas-post">Sanna’s Post</a> in March 1900 and repeatedly escaping the British columns sent to catch him.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/johannes-brand"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/johannes-brand.jpg" alt="Johannes Brand" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/johannes-brand">Johannes Brand</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:JHBrand_VA0907_bw.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown authorUnknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The constitution of 1854, which drew partly on the constitution of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, created a single-chamber <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volksraad-dutch-east-indies">Volksraad</a> elected by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burghers">burghers</a> and a president elected directly for a five-year term, assisted by an executive council. Citizenship and the vote were limited to white men. The state was divided into districts under landdrosts, with field cornets in the wards, and it developed courts, a civil service and public finances that contemporaries contrasted favourably with the turbulent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transvaal">Transvaal</a>; British and other observers often called it a model republic. Its character owed most to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johannes-brand">Johannes Henricus Brand</a>, a Cape-born lawyer who was president from 1864 until his death in 1888 and was re-elected four times. Brand steered the state through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sotho-people">Basotho</a> wars, protested by diplomacy rather than force against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>’s annexation of the diamond fields of Griqualand West in 1871, and in 1876 accepted £90,000 in compensation. He preferred good relations with the Cape to closer ties with the Transvaal and kept the Free State out of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-boer-war">First Boer War</a>. His successors, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-william-reitz">Francis William Reitz</a> and from 1896 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martinus-theunis-steyn">Martinus Theunis Steyn</a>, moved closer to the South African Republic, and in 1897 the two states concluded a defensive alliance that bound the Free State to its neighbour’s fortunes.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/national-womens-monument"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/national-womens-monument.jpg" alt="National Women's Monument" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/national-womens-monument">National Women's Monument</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bloemfontein_Women%27s_Memorial_Vil004.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Carlviloria, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Orange Free State showed that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boer</a> communities of the interior could sustain an orderly constitutional state, and its institutions, legal system and civil service passed into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orange-river-colony">Orange River Colony</a> and then the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orange-free-state-province">Province of the Orange Free State</a> in the Union of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>. Its wars with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sotho-people">Basotho</a> had lasting results: the land won west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caledon-river">Caledon River</a>, known as the Conquered Territory, remained part of the Free State, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moshoeshoe-i">Moshoeshoe</a>’s appeal to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> that became modern Lesotho. The dispute with Britain over Griqualand West marked an early stage in the struggle for the mineral wealth of the interior. In politics and memory the republic left a strong mark on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afrikaners">Afrikaner</a> nationalism. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bloemfontein">Bloemfontein</a> became the judicial capital of South <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> in 1910, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-womens-monument">National Women’s Monument</a> there, unveiled in 1913, commemorates the women and children who died in the camps of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-boer-war">Second Boer War</a>. Former Free State leaders remained prominent: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martinus-theunis-steyn">Steyn</a> was revered as an elder statesman, Christiaan de Wet was a leader of the rebellion of 1914, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/j-b-m-hertzog">J. B. M. Hertzog</a>, a judge and general of the republic, founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-party-south-africa">National Party</a> in 1914 and became prime minister of South Africa in 1924.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-paardeberg"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-paardeberg.jpg" alt="Battle of Paardeberg" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-paardeberg">Battle of Paardeberg</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Canadian_Infantry_at_Paardeberg,_1900.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown photographerUnknown photographer, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Free State’s end came through its alliance with the South African Republic. As tension between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paul-kruger">Paul Kruger</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> rose in the late 1890s, President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martinus-theunis-steyn">Steyn</a> hosted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bloemfontein-conference">Bloemfontein Conference</a> of May–June 1899 between Kruger and the British High Commissioner <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfred-milner">Alfred Milner</a>, but the talks failed. When war broke out in October 1899 the Free State honoured the 1897 alliance, although its own quarrel with Britain was limited. After early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boer</a> successes, the British counter-offensive under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-roberts-1st-earl-roberts">Lord Roberts</a> relieved <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kimberley">Kimberley</a> and trapped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piet-cronje">Piet Cronjé</a>’s army, which included many Free Staters, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-paardeberg">Paardeberg</a>, where about 4,000 men surrendered on 27 February 1900. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bloemfontein">Bloemfontein</a> fell without a fight on 13 March 1900, and in May Britain proclaimed the annexation of the territory as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orange-river-colony">Orange River Colony</a>. Steyn and de Wet refused to accept defeat and waged a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> war for two more years, during which British forces burned farms across the Free State and moved much of its population into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/concentration-camp">concentration camps</a>, where mortality was very high. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-vereeniging">Vereeniging</a> in May 1902 the Free State delegates were among the most reluctant to make peace, but the treaty signed on 31 May confirmed British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orange-river">Orange River</a> Colony received self-government in 1907 and in 1910 joined the Union of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> as a province.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Orange Free State was the smaller of the two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boer</a> republics, a farming state between the Orange and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaal-river">Vaal</a> rivers whose constitution and orderly government earned it the name of a model republic, and whose wars with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sotho-people">Basotho</a> and loyalty to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transvaal">Transvaal</a> shaped Lesotho, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afrikaners">Afrikaner</a> nationalism and modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>.</em></p>

